("sfig", "Fig14", "Fig. §F:designs§. Drying designs: (a) waste heat reused; (b) drying efficiency (latent heat over input energy) and drying electricity; (c) gross margin plus distilled-water value. HP: heat pump."),
("h2", "3.5. Sun drying versus the drying service"),
("p", "Sun drying works for mango in the North Pacific dry season: in March the probability of three dry days is {mango_feas_mar} and the grower obtains US${mango_sun_mar}/t of fresh fruit. In October the probability falls to {mango_feas_oct} and the value to US${mango_sun_oct}/t (Fig. §F:farmer§a). For banana and plantain, grown in humid zones, the probability does not exceed {banana_rainy_feas_max}% in any month from May to November and the net result is near zero or negative (Fig. §F:farmer§b–c). These probabilities are counted directly in the ERA5 daily series, which has {e_rd_lo}–{e_rd_hi} rain days per year by zone. With a 2-mm wet-day threshold, which offsets ERA5’s wet bias, March mango rises to {e2_mango_mar}, October mango to {e2_mango_oct} and the rainy-season banana maximum to {e2_banana_max}%; the conclusion holds. The Secadores Digitales service returns US${mango_sd_t}/t of mango, US${banano_sd_t}/t of banana and US${platano_sd_t}/t of plantain all year, between US${lpg_gap_lo} and US${lpg_gap_hi}/t more than an LPG dryer, whose fuel costs about US${lpg_fuel_t_water} per tonne of water evaporated. For reject banana the service more than doubles the local fresh-sale value; for plantain, which has a good fresh market, dehydration at bulk prices does not beat fresh sale and pays only for surpluses or at premium prices."),
("sfig", "Fig05", "Fig. §F:farmer§. Net value per tonne of fresh fruit by outlet and monthly probability of completing sun drying (bars): (a) mango, North Pacific; (b) banana, Caribbean; (c) plantain, Northern Zone. Probabilities counted in ERA5 2010–2025 daily precipitation."),
("p", "Coffee faces the same problem at the start of the harvest: in October and November drying patios are exposed to rain in the Northern Zone, Central Valley and Los Santos, and guardiolas burn firewood or husk. The node service offers temperature-controlled drying without fuel at US${cafe_fee_t} per tonne of wet parchment. For rainy-season grass sun drying is practically impossible, so node hay is a new product for Guanacaste cattle farming in the dry season."),
("h2", "3.6. Camarones, snapper and honey"),
("p", "The 32 nodes produce {cam_t} t/year of camarones and {snap_t} t/year of snapper, {aq_mult} times national production [29]. The {aq_mkt_t} t sold are worth about US${aq_value} million at US$9/kg (camarón sold at home), US$6/kg (exported camarón) and US$6.50/kg (snapper); the {gift_t} t for residents cost US${gift_cost} million/year in cash (US${gift_pp} per resident; Supplementary notes). They use {cam_heat} GWhₜ of heat ({aq_heat_snap} GWhₜ for snapper; {cam_kwh_lo}–{cam_kwh_hi} kWh/kg depending on altitude), {cam_elec} GWh of electricity, {ras_water} m³/year of make-up water ({ras_ls} L/s) and {salt_t} t/year of salt (US${salt_musd} million). Effluents need discharge permits: {eff_cam} m³/year of camarón water at 3 g/L and {eff_snp} m³/year of snapper brine at 32 g/L (Supplementary notes). Cross-species meals and green-banana meal ({byp_meal_t} and {bm_aq_t} t/year) supply {feed_local_pct}% of the {feed_aq_t} t/year of aquafeed. Camarón meal replaces {loop_nodes_ras_snap}% of the fishmeal protein of node snapper and snapper meal {loop_nodes_ras_cam}% of that of camarones, avoiding {loop_nodes_ras_fm} t/year of fishmeal (Table §T:loop§). With more snapper, snapper meal reaches the 50% limit in camarón feed and leaves a surplus of {loop_0p1pct_surplus}–{loop_1pct_surplus} t of protein per year, which could feed more camarones; camarón meal, in contrast, covers only {loop_1pct_snap}% of snapper needs at 37,110 t/year. {loop_actual_2020_fm}–{loop_1pct_fm} t/year of fishmeal are avoided, and meal drying uses {loop_actual_2020_heat}–{loop_1pct_heat} GWhₜ/year."),
