("h2", "3.10. Compute economics and community benefit"),
("p", "With {ec_secadores_normal_gpus} GPUs sold at US$5.10 per hour, the interruptible network earns US${ec_secadores_normal_rev} billion/year of revenue and US${ec_secadores_normal_ebitda} billion of EBITDA ({ec_secadores_normal_margin}%; Table §T:economics§, Fig. §F:econ§a). It requires US${ec_secadores_normal_capex} billion of capital and a GPU refresh reserve of US${ec_secadores_normal_refresh} billion/year, leaving US${ec_secadores_normal_cash} billion of cash. The interruptible contract sells only {ec_secadores_normal_util}% of GPU-hours; with firm supply 90% would be sold and EBITDA would rise to US${ec_secadores_firm_ebitda} billion, while rainy-season-led operation lowers it to US${ec_secadores_seasonal_ebitda} billion. That is the economic price of the carbon advantage. EBITDA is not profitability either: with a 9% capital charge of US${ec_secadores_normal_capchg} billion/year, the remaining margin is US${ec_secadores_normal_net} billion in the interruptible case, US${ec_secadores_seasonal_net} billion in the seasonal case and US${ec_secadores_firm_net} billion with firm supply, against US${ec_quantum_limon_net} billion for Quantum. In the conservative commercial case network EBITDA falls to US${cons_secadores_normal_ebitda} billion and does not cover the capital charge; the same holds for Quantum ({cons_quantum_limon_ebitda} billion). Quantum would earn US${ec_quantum_limon_ebitda} billion and Palo Negro US${ec_palo_negro_38mw_ebitda}–{ec_palo_negro_120mw_ebitda} billion. At the low or high compute price, network EBITDA ranges from US${ec_net_ebitda_low} to US${ec_net_ebitda_high} billion; the community benefit scales in proportion."),
("p", "The community benefit is paid only from cash left after the 9% capital charge (and income tax in the conservative case). In the interruptible case the full 10% of EBITDA, US${cm_normal_budget} million/year or US${cm_normal_perhh} per household in the host districts, is funded, but it takes {cm_share_headroom}% of that margin and leaves US${cm_headroom} million. It first pays for the seafood allocation (US${gift_cost} million/year, {gift_share_budget}% of the budget). Giving a free EV to each of the {cm_hh} households costs US${cm_normal_free_ev_central_cost} billion and, after paying for free charging ({cm_charging_gwh} GWh/year, US${cm_normal_charging} million), is completed in {cm_normal_free_ev_central_yrs} years at full capacity and {cm_normal_central_ev_ramp} years with the deployment ramp, {cm_normal_ev_ramp_nofish} without the seafood (price sensitivities in the Supplementary notes). Under rainy-season-led operation, in a dry year and in the conservative commercial case there is no distributable cash (Fig. §F:econ§b), so the programme, seafood included, is suspended (the US${cm_seasonal_target} million target of the rainy-season case is not funded) and resumes only when signed contracts restore a positive margin; with firm supply it takes {cm_firm_share_headroom}% of the margin. With hours and costs fixed, the full 10% needs at least US${p_full} per GPU-hour ({p_full_pct}% below the assumed price) and the margin after capital disappears below US${p_zero}; the delivery times are therefore a conditional affordability envelope, not a delivery schedule (accounts by entity in the Supplementary notes). Once the fleet is delivered, replacing each EV after 10 years and keeping charging free costs {cm_normal_steady}% of the remaining budget, leaving the rest for other benefits. The same rule would give US${cm_quantum_limon_perhh} per household in the Limón district and US${cm_palo_negro_bagaces_perhh38}–{cm_palo_negro_bagaces_perhh} per household in Bagaces, because each district hosts a whole campus; the network spreads the benefit across {cm_districts} rural districts. Free charging requires authorisation from ICE or the distributor and may be taxable as a benefit in kind; EV import exemptions should be verified before committing the programme."),
